Chapter: CamCaptureTest
  cites Works chapter CamCapture

 The pixels are what make it a capture rather than a call that returned.
 codex-vm's UVC device delivers eight vertical colour bars twenty pixels
 wide, luma alternating 16 and 235 by bar, so x=0, x=20 and x=40 sample
 three consecutive bars and pin both the content and the stride. A frame
 of zeroes -- a buffer nobody wrote -- fails all three, and so does a
 capture that timed out, because cf-ok would be False and every pixel
 read would answer zero.

 The two refusals are the other half. capture-raw at 320x240 must answer
 not-ok: this device publishes one format and the chapter refuses rather
 than resizing, so a caller asking for a size it cannot have is told.
 capture-raw at the size it CAN have must succeed, or the refusal above
 would be indistinguishable from a camera that never works.

Section: Entry

  report : CamFrame -> Text
  report (f) =
    if f.cf-ok == False then "capture: not ok"
    else "capture: ok " & show (f.cf-width) & "x" & show (f.cf-height)
      & "\nluma: " & show (frame-luma f 0 0) & " " & show (frame-luma f 20 0) & " " & show (frame-luma f 40 0)
      & "\nchroma: " & show (frame-chroma f 0 0) & " " & show (frame-chroma f 20 0) & " " & show (frame-chroma f 40 0)
      & "\nrow1: " & show (frame-luma f 0 1) & " " & show (frame-luma f 20 1)
      & "\noutside: " & show (frame-luma f 160 0) & " " & show (frame-luma f 0 120)

  opening : [Console, Camera] Nothing = act
    f <- capture
    print-line-uni (report f)
    wrong <- capture-raw 320 240
    print-line-uni ("raw 320x240 ok " & show (wrong.cf-ok))
    right <- capture-raw 160 120
    print-line-uni ("raw 160x120 ok " & show (right.cf-ok))
  end
